## Finding

- Pinned `ConsumeMessagePopOrderlyService` has one public constructor and 15
  declared public methods. Its Java service field in
  `DefaultMQPushConsumerImpl` is private and has no getter.
- The fixture now records all constructor and method signatures. Java's active
  owner calls are startup, shutdown, scheduled lock renewal and POP request
  submission. The nested `ConsumeRequest.run` ends after its dropped check and
  sharded lock lookup. `submitConsumeRequest` throws
  `UnsupportedOperationException`; `processConsumeResult` returns `true`
  without processing.
- Rust owns POP delivery and orderly lock lifecycle through `PushConsumer`,
  `OrderlyConsumeService` and its internal POP worker. It does not expose a
  separately constructible concrete POP-orderly service. No one-to-one parity
  claim is made for direct construction or helper methods not reached from the
  active request path.
